  <p>Every cell comes from Chinese press reporting the platform's own announcement or merchant rules, January 2024 to September 2026. None was read from a rule center.</p>

  <p>The New Year goods festival, nianhuo jie, shows why the year matters.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Tmall's New Year goods festival sale opened on 17 January in 2024 and on 6 January in 2025. In 2026 the spot sale opened on 4 January and closed on 11 February, four days before the nine-day Spring Festival holiday began.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Chinese press reporting the platform's festival in January 2024, January 2025 and January 2026, against the State Council General Office notice on the 2026 public holiday schedule, published 4 November 2025. The four-day gap is date arithmetic.</p>

  <p>The big two move too. Tmall's 618 opened eight days later in 2026 than in 2025. The <a href="/resources/insights/singles-day-618-production-calendar">11.11 and 618 production calendar article</a> sets out both cycles in full.</p>

  <p>No dated platform reporting was found for the September sale in 2024 or 2025, for any summer promotion, or for a brand day schedule. Tmall's 2026 Double 11 rules were reported on 22 September 2026, and the spot sale closes on 13 November, one day earlier than in 2025.</p>

  <h2>Why do published Tmall festival dates disagree?</h2>

  <p>Consumer guides outnumber press reports on every Chinese festival query, and in two recent years they printed the wrong window.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In both 2025 and 2026, Chinese consumer shopping guides printed New Year goods festival dates that did not match reporting of Tmall's own timetable: 17 to 31 January for 2025, against a reported 6 January opening, and 19 to 31 January for 2026, against a reported spot sale from 4 January to 11 February.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Chinese consumer guides and ecommerce and technology press, December 2024 to February 2026. The conflict is published rather than resolved; the platform's timetable as reported is preferred because it carries every phase.</p>

  <p>The 2025 guide window matches the 2024 festival to the day. That looks like a page carried forward. Double 12 had the same problem at the other end.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Some December 2025 consumer guides and one sponsored article gave Taobao's Double 12 an end date of 14 December. Reporting of the platform's merchant rules gave 11:59 p.m. on 12 December, and one guide listed 13 to 15 December as a separate return period.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Chinese trade reporting of the rules, 26 November 2025, against consumer guides and a sponsored article, 3 to 11 December 2025. The rules as reported are preferred; the conflict is published, not averaged.</p>

  <p>Build to the timetable reported for that year's rules. When a guide and a rules report disagree, the rules report wins.</p>

  <h2>What do Tmall's promotion rules ask of a store?</h2>

  <p>Less than most briefs assume, and nothing about creative.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">For 618 in 2025, Tmall dropped merchant-level sign-up. Stores registered products only, from a list the platform recommended based on store data, and could submit it in one batch.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Chinese financial press reporting the platform's 2025 618 rules, 8 May 2025. Rules as reported.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Tmall's 2026 618 rules, as reported, required a store experience score of at least 4.2 and no more than 24 penalty points in the previous 90 days, products with at least one unit sold in the past 60 days and a product experience score of 60 or above, presale products listed before the presale warm-up and spot products listed by 10:00 p.m. on the first day of sale, price protection and free shipping, and shipment within 48 hours of payment.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Chinese ecommerce trade press reporting the platform's 2026 618 merchant rules, 29 April 2026, republished by a Chinese technology news site the same day. Rules as reported, not read from the platform's rule center.</p>

  <p>That listing deadline is the closest thing to a content deadline in any reported rule set. It is reported for Tmall's 2026 618 only. Do not assume it for Double 11, Double 12 or the New Year goods festival.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">None of five Tmall and Taobao promotion rule sets reported in the Chinese press between May 2025 and April 2026 names a required creative asset, a store decoration refresh, a livestream or a short video. The gates they report are store and product scores, sign-up windows, listing deadlines, pricing mechanics and shipping.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: five Chinese press reports of platform merchant rules (Tmall 618 in 2025 and 2026, Tmall Double 11 2025, Taobao Double 12 2025, and the Tmall 2026 New Year goods festival), each read in full on 10 September 2026 for any content, decoration, livestream or video clause. An observation about the reporting, not a reading of the full rule text.</p>

  <p>On the reported record, store content is a sales decision, not a compliance item. The full rule text sits behind a seller login, so read each cycle's rules in your own seller backend.</p>

  <h2>How often should a Tmall store be redecorated?</h2>

  <p>No source says.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No source found publishes how often a Tmall store should be redecorated for a promotion. There is no published campaign skin specification and no published size set for event entry modules. Nine Chinese-language sources carrying store decoration sizes publish no promotion skin sizes, and none of five reported 2025 and 2026 rule summaries mentions store decoration.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a nine-source Chinese-language store decoration collection read in full on 10 September 2026, five Chinese press reports of platform merchant rules from May 2025 to April 2026, and Chinese searches for a redecoration cadence run the same day. Stated as an absence, with the search date.</p>

  <p>So the cadence is the brand's call. In production practice it follows the promotions the store joins, plus one changeover each season. The <a href="/resources/insights/tmall-flagship-store-decoration-specs">Tmall flagship store decoration specs article</a> covers the module sizes the sources do publish.</p>

  <h2>How much time sits between Tmall's promotions?</h2>

  <p>Not much, and the gaps are where production happens. Every figure here is date arithmetic on reported dates, not a published lead time.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Counted from reported dates, Taobao's 2025 Double 12 sign-up opened 11 days after Tmall's Double 11 spot sale closed on 14 November, and Tmall's 2026 New Year goods festival presale warm-up began 20 days after Double 12 closed.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: date arithmetic, 10 September 2026, on Chinese press reporting of the platform's 2025 Double 11 rules (24 September 2025), its 2025 Double 12 rules (26 November 2025) and its 2026 New Year goods festival timetable (6 January 2026). Arithmetic on reported dates, not a published lead time.</p>

  <p>The tightest squeeze is February.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In 2026, Tmall's March promotion presale opened on 24 February, the day after the nine-day Spring Festival holiday ended.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: date arithmetic, 10 September 2026, on Chinese news reporting of the platform's 2026 March promotion (25 February 2026) and the State Council General Office holiday notice (4 November 2025). Arithmetic on two dated sources, not a platform rule.</p>

  <p>March work had to be finished before the break. Spring is looser: 51 days from the March close to the reported 2026 618 rules, then 22 more to the opening. Tmall's Double 11 rules came 21 days before the presale in 2025 and 23 days before it in 2026. That is a confirmation window, not a production window.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No platform rule, government notice or survey found in September 2026 publishes how long creative production should take before a Singles Day or 618 deadline. The lead times in circulation come from companies selling preparation services, with no sample and no method behind them.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: search of Chinese- and English-language sources, 10 September 2026, covering reporting of platform rules and the pages ranking for four buyer phrasings. An observation about the published record, not a measurement.</p>

